-snip">Semen Olelkovich was the last Prince of Kiev, ruling from 1455 to 1470 as a vassal of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/semey"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/semey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Semey</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Semey, known until 2007 as Semipalatinsk, is a city on the Irtysh river in north-eastern Kazakhstan, founded as a Russian fort in 1718.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/semi-subterranean-temple"><span class="enc-name">Semi-Subterranean Temple</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Semi-Subterranean Temple is a sunken rectangular court at Tiwanaku, near Lake Titicaca in Bolivia, and among the oldest structures on the site.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/semien"><span class="enc-name">Semien</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Semien is a mountainous region of northern Ethiopia, north-east of Gondar, containing the Semien Mountains and Ras Dashen, the country's highest peak.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/semigallians"><span class="enc-name">Semigallians</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Semigallians were a Baltic people of Semigallia, the plain of central Latvia and northern Lithuania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/seminaire-de-quebec"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/seminaire-de-quebec.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Séminaire de Québec</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Séminaire de Québec is a Catholic seminary founded in 1663 by François de Laval, first bishop of Quebec, to train priests for New France.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a 