t between 1848 and 1854 to designs by Carl…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/semna"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/semna.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Semna</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Semna was a group of Egyptian fortresses at the southern end of the Second Cataract of the Nile in Nubia, in present-day Sudan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/semna-dispatches"><span class="enc-name">Semna Dispatches</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Semna Dispatches are copies of reports sent from Egyptian fortresses in Nubia, such as Semna, to the administration at Thebes in the reign of Amenemhat III.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/semnones"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/semnones.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Semnones</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Semnones were a Germanic people living between the Elbe and the Oder in the 1st and 2nd centuries CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/semois"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/semois.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Semois</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Semois is a river of the Ardennes, about 210 kilometres long, rising near Arlon in the Belgian province of Luxembourg and winding west through deep meanders past…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sempach-charter"><span class="enc-name">Sempach Charter</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sempach Charter was an agreement of 1393 among the eight Swiss Confederate cantons and Solothurn setting common rules for conduct in war.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/semu"><span class="enc-name">Semu</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Semu was a category in the social hierarchy of the Yuan Dynasty, covering peoples of Central and Western Asian origin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/semyon-dezhnev"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/semyon-dezhnev.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Semyon Dezhnev</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Semyon Dezhnev was a Russian cossack explorer of Siberia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/semyon-mikhailovich-of-novosil"><span class="enc-name">Semyon Mikhailovich of Novosil</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Semyon Mikhailovich was a Rurikid prince, a son of Mikhail of Chernigov, who was killed at the camp of Batu Khan in 1246.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sen-no-rikyu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sen-no-rikyu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sen no Rikyū</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sen no Rikyū was a Japanese tea master from a merchant family of Sakai who shaped the tea ceremony into its classic form.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sena-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sena-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sena dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sena dynasty ruled Bengal from the late 11th century to the early 13th century, after taking over from the Pala Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sena-gallica"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sena-gallica.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy"