h="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senate of Milan</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Senate of Milan was the supreme court and highest council of the Duchy of Milan, created by Louis XII of France in 1499 after his conquest of the duchy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/senchus-fer-n-alban"><span class="enc-name">Senchus fer n-Alban</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Senchus fer n-Alban ('history of the men of Scotland') is an early medieval Irish text describing the kingdom of Dál Riata in western Scotland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sendai"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sendai.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sendai</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sendai is the capital of Miyagi Prefecture and the largest city of the Tōhoku region in north-eastern Japan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sendai-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sendai-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sendai Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sendai Castle, also called Aoba Castle, was built from 1601 by Date Masamune on Mount Aoba above the city of Sendai in north-eastern Japan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senebkay"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senebkay.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senebkay</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Senebkay was an Egyptian king of the Second Intermediate Period, probably of the Abydos Dynasty, who reigned around 1650 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/seneca-falls-convention"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/seneca-falls-convention.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Seneca Falls Convention</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Seneca Falls Convention was the first women's rights convention, held in the Wesleyan Chapel at Seneca Falls, New York, on 19–20 July 1848.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/seneca-lake"><span class="enc-name">Seneca Lake</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Seneca Lake is the largest of the Finger Lakes of western New York, about 60 km long and over 180 m deep.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senegal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senegal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senegal</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Senegal is a region of westernmost Africa, named after the Senegal River and now an independent country.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senegal-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senegal-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senegal River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Senegal River flows from the Guinea highlands to the Atlantic, forming much of the border between modern Senegal and Mauritania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senegambia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senegambia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senegambia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Senegambia is the region of West Africa between the Senegal and Gambia rivers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/senekerim-hovhannes-artsruni"><span class="enc-name">Senekerim-Hovhannes Artsruni</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Senekerim-Hovhannes Artsruni was the last king of Vaspurakan, the Armenian kingdom around Lake Van, ruling from 1003.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/seneslau"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/seneslau.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Seneslau</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Seneslau was a Romanian voivode of the mid-13th century, known only from King Béla IV of Hungary's charter of 1247 to the Knights Hospitaller.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sengge-dzungar-ruler"><span class="enc-name">Sengge (Dzungar ruler)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sengge was ruler of the Dzungars from 1653 to 1670, the son and successor of Erdeni Batur.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sengge-namgyal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sengge-namgyal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sengge Namgyal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sengge Namgyal was king of Ladakh in the western Himalayas from about 1616 to 1642, the greatest ruler of the Namgyal dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sengoku-period.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sengoku period</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sengoku period, or Warring States period, was an era of almost continuous civil war in Japan from the late fifteenth to the late sixteenth century.</span></a></li></ul>
